a group of blind men heard that a  strange animal called an elephant had  been brought to the town but none of  them were aware of its shape or form so  they tried to find it and when they  found it started touching it to feel how  it looks like the first person whose  hand landed on the trunk said this being  is like a thick snake for another one  whose hand reached its ear it seemed  like a kind of a fan another person who  touched its leg said the elephant is a  pillar like a tree trunk the blind man  who placed his hand upon its side said  the elephant is a wall another who felt  its tail described it as a rope the last  felt its task stating the elephant is  like a spear this ancient sufi story is  a great way to introduce someone to the  core idea of systems thinking when we  mistake the parts for the whole we lose  perspective and miss the context in  which seemingly isolated events occur  especially when it comes to problem  solving and creative thinking  what we usually fail to grasp is that  the problem we face is part of a system  of problems and that also the problem  itself can be regarded as a system that  can be broken down into smaller problems  we are raised in an event-oriented  thinking world we see the world as a  complex succession of events rather than  as a system as a whole event-oriented  thinking assumes that each event we  experience  has a cause and that changing the cause  will correspondingly change the event  however that's the antithesis of how the  world functions famous systems thinker  donnella meadows writes in her book  thinking in systems a primer let's face  it the universe is messy it is  non-linear turbulent and chaotic it is  dynamic it spends its time in transient  behavior on its way to somewhere else  not in mathematically neat equilibria it  self organizes and evolves it creates  diversity not uniformity that's what  makes the world interesting that's what  makes it beautiful and that's what makes  it work understanding the endogenous  nature of systems and how they can shape  the fabric of our reality constitutes a  turning point in one's personal  development journey today i will attempt  to explain how a systems thinking  mindset can re-engineer the way one  approaches one's endeavors remember  always that everything you know and  everything everyone knows is only a  model get your model out there where it  can be viewed invite others to challenge  your assumptions and add their own this  is my model  when we look at the system rather than  comparing it to what we wanted to do and  then saying it is failing if it doesn't  do that we should instead look at what  it is doing well and then study how it  is designed to do that whenever you face  a setback or things don't go the way you  have planned there is absolutely no  reason to get discouraged let's say you  worked hard on a project but you  eventually failed your failure depends  on various reasons but that doesn't mean  that the system of working hard actually  failed while working hard on hard  problems you train your memory muscle  and you improve your analytical thinking  which results in overall better problem  solving ability eventually you will  succeed on a similar project and the  overall working system improves systems  thinking can help us see that  quote-unquote failing systems are  essentially a way to evolve our thinking  patterns  in our society most of the conundrums we  face are a consequence of our inability  to connect the dots in the relations  between our systems as donella meadows  writes in her book there are no separate  systems the world is a continuum let's  use an example from real life  in most countries the problem of  gank-related violence especially in  youngsters is usually attempted to be  solved by more policing because  event-oriented thinking suggests that  violence is solved by imposing order  however the problem of youngsters  joining gangs is often related to family  dysfunction and the family issues may be  related to unemployment and so on it is  a structural issue that requires a  systems thinking approach problems do  not occur in isolation but in relation  to each other this also applies to the  way we approach problem solving whenever  you feel frustrated by a problem you  face and you think you are never going  to reach  a solution try and think of other areas  that affect your problem let's say  you're facing social anxiety this is a  psychological issue that is  deeply rooted in the constitution and  psychological background of the  individual but it might also be a  byproduct of the environment that the  individual decides to inhabit maybe you  don't go out a lot maybe you don't  expose yourself to unfamiliar situations  maybe you don't partake in group  activities often enough systems thinking  can help us see that what may seem as an  isolated problem is actually part of an  interconnected network of related events  it is a critical alternative of systems  thinking to always learn from your  experiences regardless of their negative  or positive nature becoming aware of  what the system tells you every day is  an asset by learning how to evaluate the  feedback you get you develop an  awareness that allows you to learn from  what the system tells you in every  endeavor you embark on be constantly  aware of your accomplishments and try to  evaluate your progress on a regular  basis feedback coming from both yourself  and other people related to the endeavor  mainly experts not random outsiders is  endemic to the fruition of the endeavor  as you attempt to optimize the system  bear in mind that results after applying  feedback do not occur instantly you  can't navigate well in an interconnected  feedback dominated world unless you take  your eyes off short-term events and look  for long-term behavior and structure  unless you are aware of false boundaries  and bounded rationality unless you take  into account limiting factors  non-linearities and delays for instance  if we give a person medication and they  don't get better fast it would be  dangerous to give them more medication  instead of simply wait for the  medication to kick in understanding  these delays is important so that we  don't overreact during a lag period  thinking that no change is happening  when in fact it is just delayed we  usually have a bad tendency to judge  early whenever we attempt to change  something in our lives if we don't see  immediate results we get discouraged and  give up the idea of delay is crucial to  the system's thinking concept it takes  time to experience lasting optimized  results and this is an integral part of  any serious system  addiction is finding a quick and dirty  solution to the symptom of the problem  which prevents or distracts one from the  harder and longer term task of solving  the real problem whatever your personal  ambitions and goals might be when it  comes to systems thinking you need to  embrace long-term solutions instead of  quick and easy fixes long-term solutions  require the proper understanding of each  part 4. the stability of a system is  predicated on the harmony between the  parts that's such a crucial aspect of  personal development and that's why i  often suggest the adoption of a  generalist mindset the generalist  mindset urges you to delve into  different topics and eventually try to  develop an  interdisciplinary approach when it comes  to thinking and learning you start to  think in systems and this mode of  cognition leads you to deeper states of  consciousness that challenge the way you  interpret the world you become more  aware of your own existence your own  limitations and your own potential the  mechanics of the world start to make  sense and you start to perceive patterns  as philosopher isaiah berlin once said  to understand is to perceive patterns  hey guys hope you enjoyed today's video  if you did make sure to like subscribe  turn on notifications and comment below  something cool please so that more  people can discover it  if you want to watch more videos from my  channel check out this one and this one  take care see you soon editor now
 

